{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791060858,"nonce":"HsHv5YvZxmrQXLXXaam1Fh816nw=","title":"A hot reload waits for an LLVM compile in flight","message":"ashui-10 measured 3.5 s from renaming classes2.hl over a running before2.hl to\n\"[hot-reload] reloaded 116 changed function(s)\" on the NUC, against 0.10 s for\nheading2.hl. That run's log has \"[promote] findex=1401 path=own bodies=1\nmiddle_end=2978ms\"; the NUC was loaded (zyntax tests at -j6).\n\napply_reload took the LLVM tier's lock before do_reload, so a reload queued\nbehind any LLVM compile holding it. Since 4d15f3f the wait buys nothing: a\ncompile that began before the reload is discarded by tiered_compile_tier and\nthe slot it published goes back to the interpreter.\n\nNot reproduced: c783adb's binary (~/.cache/ash-nuc/ash-remap) reloaded\nclasses2.hl in 0.04-0.11 s in 10 runs on an idle-ish NUC, renaming 0.3, 1, 5\nand 10 s after \"ready\", with ASH_TIER1=20 to force early promotions. No LLVM\ncompile overlapped the reload in any of them; a compile takes ~20 ms there.\nTo confirm, rerun the slow case with ASH_TIER_LOG=1 and look for a tier=1\ncompile ending after the rename.","files":null}]}